EXTENSION "ARMDef_The_Lion_and_the_Lily_Confluensis_JeromeFollowerOfJerbiton" {
    NAME "Ars Magica Fifth Edition, The Lion and the Lily - Jerome, Follower of Jerbiton"
    VERSION "0.5.0"
    SPEC_VERSION "0.5"
    RELEASE_DATE "2026-10-06"
    DEPENDS_ON "ARMDef_The_Lion_and_the_Lily_Core_Ext"

    # Jerome, Follower of Jerbiton (Chapter Eleven: Confluensis, p138): one character, its printed stat lines verbatim fields, its write-up the DESCRIPTION.

    #tc27e25f51e3e1ad6d4e9cf ^"Jerome, Follower of Jerbiton" DEF {
        EXTENDS #tHoHTL05ExampleCharacterAA ^"Example Character"
        PROPERTIES {
            ^"Role" STRING "Magus"
            ^"Covenant" LIST [#t9b2aa72ed33f3af450ee9e ^"Confluensis"]
            ^"Age" STRING "87 (Apparent age 50)"
            ^"Personality Trait" STRING "Caring +3, Friendly +2, Modest +2"
            ^"Source" STRING "The Lion and the Lily"
            ^"Page" INTEGER 138
        }
        DESCRIPTION "Jerome is the Tribunal’s primary contact with the nobility of France. The son of minor noble, he was abandoned by a power-hungry uncle after the death of his parents. He was found near death by a Redcap and nursed back to health by a Jerbiton magus who took him as an apprentice.\n\nUnknown to Proctor, Jerome is secretly responsible for saving the lives of dozens of “troublesome heirs.” He rescues children who are likely to be killed because of their birthrights, using Mentem magic to convince people they are dead, and then places them in either in monasteries or covenants. Occasionally he finds a Gifted child this way, as he did with Artorius. He knows this is very likely a Code violation, but he continues anyway. He recalls all too clearly what it is like to have someone desire your death simply because of your birthright.\n\nJerome’s relocations cannot be accomplished without the assistance of magi from other covenants. His chief partner in this matter is his former apprentice, Eloi of Cunfin. Any character who is part of this network gains the Dark Secret flaw, but can also count Jerome as a strong ally. Jerome’s network of contacts may be ancient; for example, William the Bastard disappeared for eight years during childhood, before returning to claim first Normandy, then England.\n\nWhen not rescuing children, he takes great joy in his mediocre skill at chess. He maintains many boards in his laboratory, and plays games by letter with several far finer players. He is a popular correspondent, because he does not quit when it is clear he will lose, and plays well enough to be a challenge. He and his correspondents have agreed that one day they must seek the magical chessboard that is one of the Treasures of Britain. He is entirely undeterred by other magi who claim that “chessboard” is a poor translation for the name of an earlier, more obscure Welsh game.\n\nJerome takes pains to not dress like a nobleman. He looks much like a member of one of the preaching orders, although they generally attract younger men. He carries a wand, and has a bearded vulture as his familiar. The odd things it eats in the swamp have changed the color of its chest plumage to a mossy green.\n\nEloi, Jerome’s fillius, is a politically important member of the Covenant of Cunfin (see Chapter 9: Champagne, Cunfin)."
    }

}
